314:# Part 3: Design
318:The milestone has five pieces: two passes on `typed.blocks` (`rotate` and `inline`) and three backend options (`layout: 'loops'`, `coalesce` and `homes`). Each can be turned on alone, measured alone and reviewed alone. The three backend options share one notion of where a function spends its time, the block frequencies of section 11, which the layout uses to order blocks and which the other two use to decide whether they pay.
320:## 10. Loop rotation as header duplication (`rotate`, proposed)
322:### 10.1 The idea
341:### 10.2 Rules
343:`rotate` is a `typedPass` with `needs: { loops: 'loops' }`, `writes: ['stmts', 'values', 'cfg']`, and no preservation claims:
346:rotate(f):
360:### 10.3 When the header's test is not the exit test
366:This is valid and safe, for three reasons. The program computes the same thing, by the tail-duplication argument of 10.1. The dataflow solver widens at the target of every retreating edge of a depth-first search (`depthFirst`, `src/framework/dataflow/solver.ts:63`), and every cycle contains such an edge, so the interval analysis still terminates. And the passes that need natural loops (`licm`) simply find no loop there, so they do nothing to it. The consequence is about order: `rotate` runs after `licm` in O3. The backend does not depend on loop structure, except through block frequencies, which section 11 defines without natural loops for this reason.
368:### 10.4 What rotation alone does and does not buy
